#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Year 3 Creative Arts Written Core batch 05: class artwork, singing, instruments, composing, music cultures w25-l1 through w30-l2 (12)."""
from arts_support import L, pv, create, VIS, MUS

LESSONS = [
L('s2-arts-y3-w25-l1', VIS, ['a class artwork', 'a design brief', 'roles', 'a shared plan'],
  "A class artwork is made by many people working together on one piece. First agree on a design brief: what the artwork shows, the size, the materials and the techniques, such as printing, collage or drawing. Divide jobs so everyone has a role, such as planning the layout, preparing the materials, making sections and putting the sections together. Draw a shared plan so every section fits. Listen to each other's ideas, take turns and agree on one idea. Use one or two main colours so the artwork looks joined up.",
  "Example: a class of four plans a mural of a rainforest. Brief: one large paper, collage and printed leaves. Jobs: Ava draws the layout, Ben prints leaf patterns, Cleo collages the animals and Dev adds the border. They agree on greens, yellows and one red for the parrot, so the mural looks connected. They check the plan each lesson to see that each section fits.",
  "A group of three wants to make a class artwork of the ocean. Write the design brief in one sentence and give each person a job.",
  "Strong answer: 'We will make a collage of an ocean scene on one large sheet using blue, green and white paper. Person 1 plans the layout, person 2 makes the fish, person 3 makes the waves and plants.' A clear brief and fair jobs help the artwork fit together.",
  "Thinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared colours so the parts join together.",
  "Plan a group artwork with a family member or friends. Write the design brief, list the materials and give each person a job. Draw the layout on a large sheet. Make your section and record how you listened to the other person's idea."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w25-l2', VIS, ['a showcase', 'an exhibition', 'a label', 'a reflection'],
  "At the end of an art unit, artists display their work in an exhibition. Choose the works that best show what you have learned, arrange them so the viewer's eye can move easily and add a label to each. A label gives the title, the artist's name, the materials and a sentence about the work. Visitors respond with two stars and a wish. Afterwards write a reflection about the elements and techniques you used, what worked and what you will do next time. Handle artworks carefully by their edges.",
  "Example: Bo's label reads: 'Wet Day. Bo, Year 3. Torn paper collage. I used overlapping layers of grey and blue to show rain.' He displays it at eye level next to another collage so visitors can compare. A visitor writes, 'Star: the layers look like real rain. Wish: add a puddle texture.' Bo's reflection says he enjoyed layering and will use more texture next time.",
  "Write a label for an artwork you made this unit, including the title, materials and one sentence about what you did.",
  "Strong answer: 'Garden Pattern. Printed with a sponge and a potato on paper. I repeated shapes in rows to make a pattern.' A label gives the facts and one sentence about the work.",
  "Thinking that an exhibition must show every piece. An exhibition shows the most effective works, arranged carefully, so the viewer can enjoy each one.",
  "Choose your best three artworks and plan a small exhibition at home. Write a label for each, arrange them at eye level and invite a family member to leave two stars and a wish. Write a reflection on the techniques you learned this unit."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w26-l1', MUS, ['posture', 'breath', 'singing in tune', 'singing in time'],
  "Singing in tune means matching the pitch of the song, and singing in time means keeping to the beat. Stand or sit tall with relaxed shoulders, take a low, quiet breath into your belly and sing with a gentle, clear voice. Listen to the others and to the accompaniment. Start together on a count-in. If your voice goes flat, listen again and aim a little higher. Breathe at the end of phrases, not in the middle of words. Sip water, and stop singing if your throat feels sore.",
  "Example: Mia and Jo sing 'Row, row, row your boat'. They sit tall, take a breath before each line and tap the beat on their knees. On 'gently down the stream' Jo's voice goes too low. She listens to Mia and brings her pitch up. They finish together after a final count of 4. Their singing is in tune and in time.",
  "A singer starts too early and runs out of breath in the middle of a phrase. Write two changes that would fix this.",
  "Strong answer: 'Listen to the count-in and start on beat one with everyone. Take a breath at the end of the phrase before the next line.' A count-in keeps singers together, and planned breaths stop running out of air.",
  "Thinking that singing louder helps you stay in tune. Listening carefully and singing gently matters most, and loud singing can strain your voice.",
  "Choose a song you know well. Sing it three times: once to check your breath at the end of each line, once tapping the beat and once with a family member. Write one thing that improved. If singing is uncomfortable, hum or speak the words in rhythm."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w26-l2', MUS, ['unison', 'a round', 'a verse and chorus', 'a part'],
  "When a group sings together, they can sing in unison, which means everyone sings the same notes at the same time. In a round, such as 'Row, row, row your boat', the group splits and each part begins at a different time while singing the same tune. A song may have verses that tell the story and a chorus that repeats. To sing together well, listen to the other parts, keep the beat and watch the leader. Count in before starting, and sing at a volume so you can hear the other voices.",
  "Example: a class sings 'Row, row, row your boat' in unison first. Then the leader splits them into two groups. Group 1 starts, and Group 2 begins when Group 1 reaches 'gently down the stream'. Each group keeps its own beat and listens to the other. At the end, Group 1 finishes first and Group 2 finishes last, so the leader signals the end together.",
  "In a round, a singer gets confused by the other group's tune. Write two ways they can keep their place.",
  "Strong answer: 'Listen to my own group and sing a little softer than the others, and look at the leader's beat. I can also follow the words on a lyric card.' Staying with your own part and watching the leader helps hold your place.",
  "Believing that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard. Each group sings at the same volume so you can hear both parts.",
  "Learn a short round with a family member. Write down the words, mark where the second group begins and sing it as a round. Then sing in unison. Write which was harder and one thing that helped you keep your part."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w27-l1', MUS, ['a drum', 'a shaker', 'a xylophone', 'an instrument family'],
  "Classroom instruments come in families. Percussion instruments are hit or shaken, such as drums, tambourines, claves and shakers. Tuned percussion, such as a xylophone or glockenspiel, has bars of different lengths: short bars sound higher and long bars sound lower. Wind instruments, such as the recorder, need air. Each instrument has a way to play it correctly: hold the mallet loosely, hit the centre of the bar and let it ring. Carry instruments carefully and keep them away from water. Check with an adult before using any instrument that needs a mouth.",
  "Example: Pat plays a xylophone. He strikes a short bar and hears a high pitch, then strikes a long bar and hears a lower pitch. He uses soft mallets and bounces them off the bar so the sound rings. He sorts his group's instruments into families: drum and shaker (percussion), xylophone (tuned percussion).",
  "Sort these into families and say which xylophone bar gives the lower sound: a tambourine, a glockenspiel, a drum and a recorder.",
  "Strong answer: tambourine and drum are percussion, the glockenspiel is tuned percussion and the recorder is a wind instrument. The longer bar gives the lower sound, and the short bar gives the higher sound.",
  "Thinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest. Gentle, accurate playing gives a clearer sound, and hitting hard can damage the instrument or hurt your hands.",
  "Find three instruments or household objects that can make sounds. Sort them into families, play a pattern on each and describe the pitch and loudness. Draw each one and label how you play it correctly."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w27-l2', MUS, ['an ensemble', 'balance', 'listening', 'a conductor'],
  "An ensemble is a group playing together. To play well in a group, everyone listens, watches the leader and starts and stops together. Balance means no one instrument is too loud and covers the others. If you have a rest in your part, count it silently, because a rest is part of the music. Agree on the beat, the tempo and the dynamics before starting. A count-in gives the beat. Put the instruments down when the leader signals, so the group is quiet before moving on.",
  "Example: a group of four plays a piece: a drum plays the beat, a shaker plays ti-ti ta, a xylophone plays a melody and a tambourine plays on beat 1. The drum is too loud, so the leader asks it to play softly so the xylophone can be heard. They count in '1, 2, 3, 4', play together and finish on the leader's signal, then put down their instruments.",
  "In a group of four, the loudest player covers the melody. Write what the group can do to fix the balance.",
  "Strong answer: 'The loud player can play softer, and the melody player can play a little louder. The leader can listen and signal who should change.' Balance means every part can be heard, and the group helps each other.",
  "Believing that the best player in a group should play loudest. Good ensemble playing means supporting the others and keeping a balance so every part can be heard.",
  "Make a four-part ensemble with household objects or body percussion. Assign a part to each player, with at least one rest. Agree on tempo and dynamics, rehearse three times and note which change improved the balance."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w28-l1', MUS, ['a shaker', 'a rubber-band guitar', 'a pitch change', 'a safe instrument'],
  "You can make simple instruments from clean recycled materials. A shaker is a container with dry rice or beans inside, sealed tightly. A rubber-band guitar is a box with rubber bands stretched across. A shorter, tighter or thinner rubber band gives a higher pitch, and a longer, looser or thicker one gives a lower pitch. A water bottle instrument changes pitch with the water level when you tap it. Use plastic, not glass. Seal everything securely and ask an adult to help cut or tape anything tricky.",
  "Example: Lily makes a rubber-band guitar with an empty tissue box and three bands of different thickness. The thin band makes the highest pitch and the thick band makes the lowest. She tightens the middle one to make it higher and checks that the bands sit securely and do not snap. She also seals a rice shaker with strong tape.",
  "You want a rubber-band guitar to play a high note and a low note. Which rubber bands would you use for each, and how can you make the high note even higher?",
  "Strong answer: 'A thin, short, tight band for the high note and a thick, long, loose band for the low note. To make the high one higher, stretch it tighter.' Thinner, shorter and tighter bands vibrate faster and sound higher.",
  "Thinking that bigger sounds mean higher notes. Size and loudness are different from pitch, because a thick band sounds lower than a thin one even if you pluck both hard.",
  "Make two instruments from recycled materials, such as a shaker and a rubber-band guitar. Draw a plan with the materials, test the pitch of each band and write which gives high or low sounds. Ask an adult to help and use only plastic and cardboard."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w28-l2', MUS, ['a design', 'testing', 'an improvement', 'playing a pattern'],
  "After you make an instrument, test it, improve it and play a pattern on it. Testing means making different sounds and listening: Is it loud or soft? High or low? Does it ring or sound dull? An improvement changes one thing, such as adding more beans for a louder shaker or loosening a band for a lower pitch. Then play a short rhythm pattern with a steady beat. Always keep instruments tidy and store them safely. Remember that a homemade instrument can still make a good sound.",
  "Example: Zane's shaker sounds dull because there are only five beans. He adds a handful more and tests again. The sound is much louder, so he tapes the lid tightly. He plays a pattern of shake, shake, rest, shake to a steady beat. His note says: 'More beans make it louder. I need to test one change at a time.'",
  "Your bottle drum sounds dull. Write one test and one improvement you could make, and say how you will know it worked.",
  "Strong answer: 'Test by tapping with a soft mallet on the lid and the side. Improvement: use a different part of the bottle or use a firmer mallet. It worked if the sound rings longer.' Changing one thing at a time shows what made the difference.",
  "Believing that if the first version sounds bad the instrument has failed. Makers test and improve, and changing one thing at a time shows what works.",
  "Test your instrument from last lesson, make one improvement and record before and after in a table. Play a four-beat rhythm on it. Perform your pattern for family and write one thing you would change in the next version."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w29-l1', MUS, ['a composition', 'a theme', 'a graphic score', 'a form (ABA)'],
  "Composing is making your own music. Start with a theme, such as 'a thunderstorm' or 'a playground'. Choose sounds that fit it, decide the beat, tempo, dynamics and pitch and order them in sections. A common form is ABA: section A, then a different section B, then A again, which makes the piece feel complete. You can write the piece as a graphic score, using pictures and symbols to show when sounds start, stop and change. Record the plan so you and others can play it again the same way.",
  "Example: Mina composes 'Playground' in ABA form. Section A: a quick, light shaker pattern. Section B: loud drum taps for a game of tag. Section A again: the shaker pattern returns, slowing down. Her graphic score uses small dots for shakes, big circles for drum hits and a line getting thinner for the ritardando.",
  "Plan a short ABA composition about a rainy day. Say what happens in section A, section B and the return of A.",
  "Strong answer: 'A: soft, slow finger taps like drizzle. B: loud shaking and stamps for heavy rain. A: soft taps again as the rain stops.' It has a clear contrast and returns to the first idea, which finishes the piece.",
  "Thinking that composing means playing whatever comes to mind. A composition has a plan: a theme, chosen sounds, a form and a way to record it so it can be played again.",
  "Compose a 30-second ABA piece with a theme of your choice. Draw a graphic score showing sounds, loudness and speed. Rehearse it twice and note two choices you made about dynamics or tempo."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w29-l2', MUS, ['a performance', 'a count-in', 'a conductor', 'a recording'],
  "To perform a composition, rehearse until each part is secure. Check the score, decide who leads and count in so everyone begins together. Keep the beat steady, follow the dynamics and tempo written in the score and finish together on a clear signal. After the performance, listen to the response from the audience. If you can record it with an adult's permission, listen back to hear the dynamics and tempo. Reflect on what matched your plan and what you would change.",
  "Example: Ravi leads his group's piece 'Playground'. He counts '1, 2, 3, 4', and they play section A softly. For B he raises his hand for a crescendo, and the group plays louder. He lowers his hand for the return of A. They finish on his closed fist. Afterwards they listen to a recording and notice the crescendo was too quick, so they plan to slow it.",
  "Write the steps for a leader to start, conduct and finish a group composition.",
  "Strong answer: 'Count in, give a clear hand cue for each change, keep the beat steady and close the hand to finish.' A clear count-in, cues and finish keep the group together.",
  "Believing that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve.",
  "Rehearse and perform your composition from last lesson for family. Use a count-in and a finish signal. Ask them for two stars and a wish and, with permission, record it to listen to. Write one change you would make."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w30-l1', MUS, ['culture', 'a tradition', 'respectful listening', 'a published source'],
  "Music is made in every culture and shows what people value and how they live. Steel pans are tuned drums from Trinidad and Tobago, made from steel drums. Taiko are large Japanese drums played with sticks. The djembe is a drum from West Africa played with the hands. Listen to the pieces with respect, find out where and why the music is made and use published, reliable sources. Music from First Nations peoples may have particular protocols, so only use approved recordings and ask local Aboriginal educators for guidance. Never mock or imitate a culture's music.",
  "Example: Tia listens to a steel pan piece from a recording chosen by her teacher. She writes: 'The music comes from Trinidad and Tobago. The tempo is lively and the pitches are bright and high. It makes me feel cheerful.' She names the place, two elements and a feeling. She also writes a question for a local community member: 'What occasions is this music played for?'",
  "Choose a piece of music from another culture. Write two respectful things to do before and while listening, and two elements you might describe.",
  "Strong answer: 'Find the place and the purpose from a published source, listen quietly to the whole piece. I will describe the tempo and the instruments.' Researching, listening quietly and using music elements show respect.",
  "Thinking that all music from a country sounds the same. Each country has many styles and traditions, and learning about one example does not tell you about all of them.",
  "With an adult, find two pieces from different cultures in a library or on an approved website. For each write the place, the instrument or voice, two elements and a feeling. Write one respectful question you would ask someone from that culture."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w30-l2', MUS, ['a showcase', 'a programme', 'an introduction', 'a reflection'],
  "A music showcase gathers your best work: singing, playing or composing. A programme lists the items in order, with the title and the performers. Before each piece, a short introduction tells the audience what to listen for, such as 'This piece uses a crescendo.' Perform with a count-in and finish together. The audience claps at the end. Afterwards, reflect on the elements you used and what you would improve. Always treat every performer with kindness and give feedback as two stars and a wish.",
  "Example: Lena's programme has three items: 1. Row, Row, Row Your Boat (round, class); 2. Playground (composition, Mina and Ravi); 3. A steel pan listening piece. Before the second item, Mina says, 'Listen for the quick shakers in section A.' The audience listens, claps and gives two stars and a wish.",
  "Write a three-item programme for a music showcase and a one-sentence introduction for one item.",
  "Strong answer: '1. Round (class), 2. Rain composition (me), 3. Body percussion piece (me and a friend). Introduction: This piece uses a crescendo from soft to loud.' The introduction names an element to listen for.",
  "Thinking that you must perform everything you know. A showcase shows a few well-prepared pieces and tells the audience what to listen for.",
  "Prepare a music showcase of three items from this unit for family. Write the programme and an introduction for each item. Perform, then collect two stars and a wish and write a reflection about the elements you used."),
]

